WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.229 --> 00:00:04.270
The opening trap for Saw 5 is the Pendulum,
the imitation Jigsaw trap created by Mark

00:00:04.270 --> 00:00:06.280
Hoffman to execute his sister’s killer.

00:00:06.280 --> 00:00:10.190
Like many Jigsaw traps, Hoffman would take
inspiration from a historical torture device

00:00:10.190 --> 00:00:12.249
and add a modern element using machinery.

00:00:12.249 --> 00:00:16.200
There’s some debate as to whether or not
The Pendulum was actually used to scare or

00:00:16.200 --> 00:00:20.550
torture prisoners of the Spanish Inquisition
in the 18th Century, but we can say with certainty

00:00:20.550 --> 00:00:24.759
that it was used by Edgar Allen Poe in his
fictional story, The Pit and The Pendulum.

00:00:24.759 --> 00:00:28.949
In fact, almost all of the traps in Saw 5
have ties to uncivilized methods of enforcing

00:00:28.949 --> 00:00:32.210
justice and the end of the movie would bring
the connection to The Pit and The Pendulum

00:00:32.210 --> 00:00:35.960
back, front and center, so stick around to
the end of this video to find out why.

00:00:40.140 --> 00:00:43.300
Welcome back to Things You Missed, and welcome
back to my Saw series.

00:00:43.309 --> 00:00:47.899
If you want to see all 171 Things You Missed
in Saws 1-4, click here.

00:00:47.899 --> 00:00:51.680
Saw 5 would continue the tradition of not
bringing in anyone with any experience directing

00:00:51.680 --> 00:00:56.129
a major studio movie, by hiring the production
designer of the last three movies, David Hackl.

00:00:56.129 --> 00:01:00.340
As you might expect, this resulted in a lot
of Easter Eggs hidden in the actual set.

00:01:00.340 --> 00:01:03.500
Maybe more than ever before, so let's take
a look at some Things You Missed.

00:01:09.300 --> 00:01:12.760
After the introduction with The Pendulum Trap,
we are finally shown the aftermath of the

00:01:12.770 --> 00:01:15.590
Gideon Meat Packing Plant games of Saw 3 and
4.

00:01:15.590 --> 00:01:18.870
Agent Strahm ends up locked in the operation
room, and is only able to find his way out

00:01:18.870 --> 00:01:22.000
with the aid of some glow in the dark paint
pointing out a secret door.

00:01:22.000 --> 00:01:25.160
This is kind of a combination of the glow
in the dark paint illuminating the breakable

00:01:25.160 --> 00:01:30.050
wall in Saw 1 and the cracks at the edge of
the trap door revealing an exit in Saw 2.

00:01:30.050 --> 00:01:33.620
Strahm then finds himself in The Water Cube
trap, and has to use the only thing he has

00:01:33.620 --> 00:01:36.180
on him, his pen, to perform a tracheotomy.

00:01:36.180 --> 00:01:39.930
This was really well set up in Saw 4, where
Strahm was seen constantly fidgeting with

00:01:39.930 --> 00:01:40.930
the pen.

00:01:40.930 --> 00:01:43.860
To Hoffman’s surprise, he makes it out of
the building alive, and on the ambulance is

00:01:43.860 --> 00:01:47.800
what I believe is the first example where
we clearly see the state license plate, and

00:01:47.800 --> 00:01:50.270
the nickname of their state is: The Fairweather
State.

00:01:50.270 --> 00:01:53.250
Throughout this series I’ve talked about
how I think Saw takes place in a fictional

00:01:53.250 --> 00:01:57.110
state in the Northeast United States and the
plate does kind of look like New Jersey.

00:01:57.110 --> 00:02:01.180
Additionally, when we see the inside of the
FBI headquarters, the words, “Eastern Surveillance”

00:02:01.180 --> 00:02:05.380
are written on a window as if to suggest Jigsaw’s
city is on the east coast, and a line from

00:02:05.380 --> 00:02:09.860
Brit Stevenson later on could be alluding
to the fact that she works nearby in New York City.

00:02:11.840 --> 00:02:12.880
"No I'm not.

00:02:14.400 --> 00:02:17.980
I build condos in one of the most densely
populated cities in the country.”

00:02:17.980 --> 00:02:22.280
The FBI has offices in both Newark and New
York, and Agent Strahm’s phone number has

00:02:22.280 --> 00:02:26.420
a 212 area code, meaning he could be from
the nearby city of New York.

00:02:26.420 --> 00:02:30.159
As Jill Tuck waits to meet with her ex-husband’s
executor, a woman in the waiting room reads

00:02:30.159 --> 00:02:34.761
a magazine about John Kramer, with articles
written by Elis Lam and Nate Richmond, which

00:02:34.761 --> 00:02:37.099
are both Easter Eggs for crew members on Saw
5.

00:02:37.099 --> 00:02:40.379
Nate Richmond was a production assistant,
and Elis Lam was the art director.

00:02:40.379 --> 00:02:44.269
As far as characters who never appear on screen,
Elis’s is one of the biggest, because she

00:02:44.269 --> 00:02:48.610
not only wrote this magazine article, but
also the newspaper article announcing Jigsaw’s

00:02:48.610 --> 00:02:52.390
death, the article about the death of Hoffman’s
sister, another article about Angelina’s

00:02:52.390 --> 00:02:55.409
murder, and the one about the death of her
killer, Seth Baxter.

00:02:55.409 --> 00:02:59.310
But there are a couple unseen easter egg characters
that are even more interesting than Elis.

00:02:59.310 --> 00:03:03.319
You may remember Sean Scoffield, the graphic
designer who put his name on the fingerprint

00:03:03.319 --> 00:03:04.950
analysis report in Saw 4.

00:03:04.950 --> 00:03:08.739
For Saw 5, he was promoted to First Assistant
Art Director, so he got to put his name in

00:03:08.739 --> 00:03:09.739
there even more.

00:03:09.739 --> 00:03:13.980
He seems to have helped prepare these probable
victim binders, along with Stephanie Woloshyn.

00:03:13.980 --> 00:03:18.040
In the real world, Stephanie is a production
assistant, but in the Saw world she’s an

00:03:18.040 --> 00:03:22.290
FBI agent living a double life as an investigative
journalist for The Herald, the same newspaper

00:03:22.290 --> 00:03:24.480
that Charles Salomon also writes for.

00:03:24.480 --> 00:03:28.719
“I’m an investigative journalist for The
Herald.”

00:03:28.719 --> 00:03:32.420
I can’t imagine the FBI is too happy about
one of their agents publishing information

00:03:32.420 --> 00:03:37.279
about the same cases that they are investigating
in a public newspaper, but for our purposes,

00:03:37.279 --> 00:03:41.599
Stephanie’s information answers a big question
about the link between John Kramer and Hoffman.

00:03:41.599 --> 00:03:45.400
How did John find out about Hoffman before
The Pendulum if The Pendulum was Hoffman’s

00:03:45.400 --> 00:03:46.889
first attempt at a trap?

00:03:46.889 --> 00:03:50.930
It seems that before killing Angelina, Seth
Baxter was involved in a drug charge, so he

00:03:50.930 --> 00:03:54.580
probably had ties to Jill’s drug clinic
like so many of Jigsaw’s victims did.

00:03:54.580 --> 00:03:58.329
So John would have been watching Seth around
the time he took out Hoffman’s sister, and

00:03:58.329 --> 00:04:01.140
when Seth went to prison for it, John switched
his focus to Hoffman.

00:04:01.140 --> 00:04:04.579
The lore you can discover in the newspapers
in this series is just so interesting to me

00:04:04.579 --> 00:04:07.520
for some reason; I guess I feel like I’m
getting extra content somehow.

00:04:07.520 --> 00:04:11.749
For example, Sean Scoffield may have also
had a second job outside the FBI, though it’s

00:04:11.749 --> 00:04:13.040
kind of hard to tell on this one.

00:04:13.040 --> 00:04:14.499
It might say Sean Medfield.

00:04:14.499 --> 00:04:18.450
We’ll have to revisit this one if they ever
release the movies in 4K… or we could just

00:04:18.450 --> 00:04:20.440
not do that because no one cares.

00:04:21.320 --> 00:04:24.560
I like how there’s a level of consistency
when building the Saw world.

00:04:24.570 --> 00:04:27.860
When Strahm is clicking through the headlines,
the publications are many of the same ones

00:04:27.860 --> 00:04:29.310
we’ve seen in previous movies.

00:04:29.310 --> 00:04:33.071
It’s also amazing how so many of these Jigsaw
articles all use this same picture, which

00:04:33.071 --> 00:04:35.360
is really just a screenshot from Saw 1.

00:04:35.360 --> 00:04:37.200
Heck I even used it in my Things You Missed
thumbnail.

00:04:37.200 --> 00:04:39.490
It’s a good picture, Billy should make it
his Twitter avatar.

00:04:39.490 --> 00:04:42.260
He’d fit right in on that website considering
he’s a puppet.

00:04:44.320 --> 00:04:47.220
When Jill watches the tape left behind for
her by John, you can tell it was made in the

00:04:47.220 --> 00:04:51.120
operating room from Saw 3 while John still
has his hair, but does not have the bruises

00:04:51.130 --> 00:04:55.370
given to him by Matthews anymore, which means he probably recorded this shortly before the Gideon Meat

00:04:55.370 --> 00:04:57.450
Packing Plant games of 2006.

00:04:57.450 --> 00:05:00.890
This is important, because it shows us how
late in his life John began to have remorse

00:05:00.890 --> 00:05:02.410
about the way his marriage ended.

00:05:02.410 --> 00:05:06.030
With the discovery of John’s body, a ceremony
is held at the police station to honor the

00:05:06.030 --> 00:05:09.080
fallen officers in this case, and to promote
Hoffman to deputy-lieutenant.

00:05:11.000 --> 00:05:15.880
“But today I’d like to offer a special
honor to the Detective who’s been on this

00:05:15.890 --> 00:05:17.440
case right from the beginning.”

00:05:17.440 --> 00:05:21.460
Wait… hold on, if you’re honoring Hoffman
for being there from the beginning, what about Fisk?

00:05:21.460 --> 00:05:25.180
According to the FBI’s records, Fisk has
actually been on the task force longer than Hoffman.

00:05:25.220 --> 00:05:29.140
I know Hoffman probably gets some credit for
saving that girl, but as far as we know, Fisk

00:05:29.140 --> 00:05:30.730
has a much cleaner record than Hoffman.

00:05:30.730 --> 00:05:32.190
He doesn’t even get honored at all.

00:05:32.190 --> 00:05:33.600
He just has to sit there.

00:05:33.680 --> 00:05:34.700
Hashtag justice for Fisk!

00:05:36.600 --> 00:05:40.720
Also, we get our first look at Pamela Jenkins,
who would play a bigger role, but not until

00:05:40.720 --> 00:05:41.600
the next movie.

00:05:41.600 --> 00:05:42.600
Foreshadowing!

00:05:42.660 --> 00:05:46.580
When Hoffman gives his speech, he talks about
cherishing life and bringing justice, which

00:05:46.590 --> 00:05:50.560
could be what you want to hear from your new
lieutenant, but also happens to be Jigsaw’s

00:05:50.560 --> 00:05:53.660
motto, and Hoffman would be the one to carry
out Jigsaw’s work.

00:05:53.660 --> 00:05:57.820
Justice would be the key theme in the main
game of Saw 5, and each trap was carefully

00:05:57.820 --> 00:06:00.020
chosen to be symbolic of this idea.

00:06:00.020 --> 00:06:02.880
“We both deserve to be here.”

00:06:12.580 --> 00:06:17.160
Strahm, or as those close to him call him,
Peter, is ordered off of the Jigsaw case by

00:06:17.160 --> 00:06:19.170
his superior, J Jonah Jameson.

00:06:19.170 --> 00:06:20.220
“Hoffman!”

00:06:20.220 --> 00:06:23.060
And Hoffman begins leaving breadcrumbs to
make Agent Jameson…

00:06:23.060 --> 00:06:26.860
I mean, Erickson, put the pieces together
that maybe Peter is Spiderman.

00:06:27.520 --> 00:06:28.560
I mean, Jigsaw.

00:06:28.570 --> 00:06:32.100
Hoffman goes back to his new lair to check
on the next game, and we see how much he’s

00:06:32.100 --> 00:06:34.620
learned from Jigsaw based on the way he decorates
the place.

00:06:34.620 --> 00:06:38.340
The anatomical human drawings call back to
the diagrams from the operation room.

00:06:38.340 --> 00:06:42.250
One of them is a copy of the vascular system
diagram from the Spike Trap in Saw 4.

00:06:42.250 --> 00:06:45.622
The miniature version of the game was also
used by John to plot out the details of the

00:06:45.622 --> 00:06:46.650
Bathroom game.

00:06:46.650 --> 00:06:49.900
This game would be known as the Fatal Five’s
trial, and because it was being held this

00:06:49.900 --> 00:06:53.930
time by Hoffman, a police officer who is obsessed
with justice after feeling his sister’s

00:06:53.930 --> 00:06:58.160
killer was not properly punished, each trap
resembles an old, outdated criminal execution

00:06:58.160 --> 00:06:58.660
method.

00:06:58.680 --> 00:07:02.620
And yes, to be entirely clear, this game was
mapped out by John, but Hoffman was still

00:07:02.620 --> 00:07:05.990
the one to put everything into motion, so
I think the symbolism is still valid.

00:07:05.990 --> 00:07:09.990
The first room is an update on a device tracing
back to France during the times of the French

00:07:09.990 --> 00:07:10.620
Revolution.

00:07:10.620 --> 00:07:14.580
The guillotine was used to behead its victims,
but this version implemented a system of tension

00:07:14.590 --> 00:07:18.420
wires to force the participants to work together
in order for them all to survive.

00:07:18.420 --> 00:07:22.150
Instead of the typical shape for a guillotine,
these blades all have a V-shape, which may

00:07:22.150 --> 00:07:26.670
be on purpose to stay on theme with the roman
numeral representing the fifth movie.

00:07:26.670 --> 00:07:30.200
There are also five participants in this game
and I don’t think that is an accident.

00:07:30.200 --> 00:07:34.040
The second room requires the victims to find
keys that give them access to these safety

00:07:34.040 --> 00:07:34.580
chambers.

00:07:34.580 --> 00:07:36.040
The name of the game is inclusion.

00:07:36.040 --> 00:07:39.560
There aren’t enough chambers to protect
everybody, so in order for everyone to survive,

00:07:39.560 --> 00:07:42.200
someone would have had to invite another into
their chamber.

00:07:42.200 --> 00:07:46.250
The Salem Witch Trials were a series of hearings
that outcast supposed witches from society

00:07:46.250 --> 00:07:50.450
in the 1600s, and like the unlucky loser who
was outcast in this room, they would meet

00:07:50.450 --> 00:07:51.530
a fiery fate.

00:07:51.530 --> 00:07:55.240
I guess this is Saw 5’s Pig Vat in the sense
that… it’s pretty metal.

00:07:55.360 --> 00:08:05.280
*METAL MUSIC*

00:08:05.280 --> 00:08:06.000
“Yeah.

00:08:06.120 --> 00:08:07.140
It is actually.”

00:08:07.140 --> 00:08:07.960
“Metal.”

00:08:07.960 --> 00:08:12.280
The third room contained the electric bathtub,
which represented another American execution

00:08:12.280 --> 00:08:16.670
method: The Electric Chair, which was created
in the 1880s in Buffalo, NY; not too far away

00:08:16.670 --> 00:08:18.140
from where I think Saw takes place.

00:08:18.140 --> 00:08:21.810
Again, this one was designed for each of the
five of them to work together by dividing

00:08:21.810 --> 00:08:25.180
up the voltage rather than having one person
take on the full shock.

00:08:25.180 --> 00:08:28.660
The last room represents human sacrifice,
which I don’t think we can trace back to

00:08:28.670 --> 00:08:32.839
one specific era or culture, but again is
designed for them to work together, by each

00:08:32.839 --> 00:08:34.729
sacrificing a smaller amount of blood.

00:08:34.729 --> 00:08:37.979
Of course, that doesn’t happen, because
there are only two of them left at this point,

00:08:37.979 --> 00:08:39.129
so here’s what I would have done.

00:08:39.129 --> 00:08:42.610
They had to fill this beaker up with blood
to a certain point in order to win the game.

00:08:42.610 --> 00:08:46.079
There is a grate blocking the way, so you
can’t just drop stuff directly in the beaker,

00:08:46.079 --> 00:08:47.180
it has to be a liquid.

00:08:47.180 --> 00:08:50.070
The door to the bathtub room was still open
after they cleared it.

00:08:50.070 --> 00:08:53.370
So I would take off my shirt, dip it in the
tub until it’s soaking wet, then ball it

00:08:53.370 --> 00:08:55.500
up and squeeze out the water above the beaker.

00:08:55.500 --> 00:08:57.769
They probably could have filled it most of
the way up like that.

00:08:57.769 --> 00:09:01.230
It’s not until the end that they realize
what Jigsaw was trying to tell them from the

00:09:01.230 --> 00:09:03.320
beginning, that they need to work together.

00:09:04.300 --> 00:09:09.400
“Today, this singular way of thinking will
be put to the test.

00:09:09.400 --> 00:09:14.020
Today, five will become one, with the common
goal of survival.”

00:09:14.020 --> 00:09:18.540
But while “Hoffman” executes the Fatal
Five’s game, Strahm follows the trail of Hoffman.

00:09:18.700 --> 00:09:23.060
In one scene at the FBI headquarters, we actually
see someone studying audio waveforms, something

00:09:23.060 --> 00:09:24.880
that would come back in the next movie.

00:09:24.880 --> 00:09:28.720
There’s also a name randomly written on
an FBI bulletin board: Tom Vencelides.

00:09:28.720 --> 00:09:31.269
He’s the assistant production manager on
Saw 5.

00:09:31.269 --> 00:09:35.430
The police station also has another more crew-reference
easter egg, a box in the background here contains

00:09:35.430 --> 00:09:38.790
the name Sorin Popescu, who is the first assistant
art director.

00:09:38.790 --> 00:09:42.920
As Strahm gets closer and closer to discovering
the truth about Hoffman, we learn more and

00:09:42.920 --> 00:09:46.279
more about Hoffman’s past and how he came
to be involved with Jigsaw.

00:09:46.279 --> 00:09:50.110
It starts after he conducted the Pendulum
trap one day at his apartment complex, when

00:09:50.110 --> 00:09:53.709
he bumps into this stereotypical girl who
probably watches my videos, and follows his

00:09:53.709 --> 00:09:54.990
journey through the building.

00:09:54.990 --> 00:09:57.810
He hears what he thinks is a dispute and approaches
with his gun out.

00:09:57.810 --> 00:10:01.329
It turns out to only be this lady with her
dog, but I think the scene does a good job

00:10:01.329 --> 00:10:03.720
of illustrating what kind of cop Hoffman is.

00:10:03.720 --> 00:10:05.670
This trait would be explored again in Saw
7.

00:10:05.670 --> 00:10:07.309
Yeah I prefer to call it Saw 7.

00:10:07.309 --> 00:10:09.899
Because it’s very difficult to watch it
in 3D anymore.

00:10:09.899 --> 00:10:13.829
And also it’s not the final chapter anymore,
so I don’t call it that either.

00:10:13.829 --> 00:10:16.970
Finally, he gets to the elevator, where he’s
gotta get out of the way of this lady, and

00:10:16.970 --> 00:10:20.399
these dudes who are probably on their way
to go meet up with FoundFlix at the Papa Roach

00:10:20.399 --> 00:10:21.399
concert.

00:10:21.399 --> 00:10:24.170
So it ends up being just Hoffman and John
in the elevator, and since this scene takes

00:10:24.170 --> 00:10:27.730
place shortly before the events of Saw 1,
John is seen wearing the leather gloves that

00:10:27.730 --> 00:10:30.899
were a big part of the first movie, but weren’t
really used in the Bousman films.

00:10:30.899 --> 00:10:35.259
Some say those gloves are an homage to Italian
horror director Dario Argento, who has a movie

00:10:35.259 --> 00:10:37.519
called Deep Red where the bad guy has these
leather gloves.

00:10:37.519 --> 00:10:41.939
I mean I guess it could be an intentional
reference, but it could just as easily be

00:10:41.940 --> 00:10:43.240
not that.

00:10:43.240 --> 00:10:44.420
“Hoffman!”

00:10:44.420 --> 00:10:48.949
He wakes up in John’s workshop in this spectacularly
uncreative trap, but one thing I did notice

00:10:48.949 --> 00:10:51.569
was an early version of the death collar from
Saw 3.

00:10:51.569 --> 00:10:55.410
In that movie, Amanda claims she built the
collar, so maybe she physically built it off

00:10:55.410 --> 00:10:57.480
of one of John’s prototype designs.

00:10:57.480 --> 00:11:01.029
When John details the rules of the game to
Hoffman, he does so through the reflection

00:11:01.029 --> 00:11:02.029
of a mirror.

00:11:02.029 --> 00:11:03.600
“I am the man you call Jigsaw.”

00:11:04.060 --> 00:11:08.060
Could he be doing this because he’s looking
into the past, looking back in the rearview

00:11:08.060 --> 00:11:11.920
at the old Hoffman who has not yet been reborn
as a disciple of Jigsaw?

00:11:11.920 --> 00:11:13.360
Is that too deep for a Saw movie?

00:11:13.840 --> 00:11:14.340
Maybe.

00:11:14.580 --> 00:11:15.340
Maybe not.

00:11:15.640 --> 00:11:19.700
Also in this workshop, I spotted one of those
Chinese figurines, from beside John’s deathbed

00:11:19.709 --> 00:11:22.620
in Saw 3 and the New Years festival in Saw
4.

00:11:22.620 --> 00:11:26.480
Strahm would continue to go through old files
to dig out more and more about Mark Hoffman’s

00:11:26.480 --> 00:11:30.790
past, where he would page past a curious detail
that could have changed the context of crimes

00:11:30.790 --> 00:11:33.780
that took place all the way back in the first
Saw film.

00:11:44.280 --> 00:11:47.680
As Strahm goes through the old case files
from the Razor Wire Trap’s victim, Paul

00:11:47.680 --> 00:11:52.020
Leahy, you may notice that Detective Hoffman
was actually the lead investigator on it.

00:11:52.020 --> 00:11:55.959
When we see that crime scene back in the first
movie, it’s Sing, Tapp and Kerry, who has

00:11:55.959 --> 00:11:57.519
not yet been promoted to detective.

00:11:57.519 --> 00:11:59.050
There’s no sign of Hoffman at all.

00:11:59.050 --> 00:12:02.149
I don’t know if that means he had already
investigated it and these other guys were

00:12:02.149 --> 00:12:05.370
coming to take a second look, or if he was
calling the shots and asked Sing and Tapp

00:12:05.370 --> 00:12:09.259
to make their own assessment, perhaps to lead
them down the path to Jigsaw with a plan to

00:12:09.260 --> 00:12:10.140
take them out.

00:12:10.140 --> 00:12:13.980
The files seen earlier on list Hoffman, Tapp
and Sing as the investigators on the case,

00:12:13.990 --> 00:12:17.850
and it seems to be the first Jigsaw case any
of them worked on from what we can see there,

00:12:17.850 --> 00:12:21.639
though I should add that there are other Jigsaw
murders that aren’t listed here or shown

00:12:21.639 --> 00:12:24.470
in any movie, but I’m not gonna talk about
that in this video.

00:12:24.470 --> 00:12:27.880
We’ll probably never solve the mystery of
Hoffman’s involvement in the Paul Leahy

00:12:27.880 --> 00:12:28.380
case.

00:12:28.380 --> 00:12:32.680
Saw 5 also has another ambiguous question
which comes up when Jill Tuck meets with Erickson

00:12:32.680 --> 00:12:36.139
at the FBI headquarters claiming that she
believes the Bureau is following her.

00:12:36.139 --> 00:12:38.360
“I think it’s the Agent who interrogated
me.”

00:12:38.360 --> 00:12:39.470
“Agent Strahm?”

00:12:39.470 --> 00:12:41.850
The strange thing is, we never see this happen.

00:12:41.850 --> 00:12:46.249
In fact, we see Strahm laser-focused on investigating
Hoffman, paying no attention to Jill.

00:12:46.249 --> 00:12:49.920
I guess it is possible that she was told to
do this by John as part of his instructions

00:12:49.920 --> 00:12:53.850
for her after his death, but I’ve come to
think there is another possibility.

00:12:53.850 --> 00:12:57.620
In Saw 6, we see she has made contact with
Hoffman, who is actively trying to get rid

00:12:57.620 --> 00:13:00.019
of Strahm and make him look like the unknown
accomplice.

00:13:00.019 --> 00:13:04.079
I think there’s a possibility that Hoffman
instructed Jill to bring up these concerns

00:13:04.079 --> 00:13:06.810
with Erickson; maybe he even threatened her
to get what he wants.

00:13:06.810 --> 00:13:10.030
I don’t see any other reason for her to
go to him, unless she’s just trying to get

00:13:10.030 --> 00:13:11.240
a job at the Daily Bugle.

00:13:11.240 --> 00:13:11.740
"You serious?"

00:13:11.840 --> 00:13:15.220
Maybe it was all part of Hoffman’s plan
to frame Special Agent Strahm.

00:13:15.220 --> 00:13:18.670
He does so by leaving a Strahm’s phone in
his lair at the catacombs where the Fiendish

00:13:18.670 --> 00:13:19.760
Five’s game took place.

00:13:20.400 --> 00:13:22.420
OK, not the Fiendish Five, the Fatal Five.

00:13:22.420 --> 00:13:23.480
That’s what they’re called.

00:13:23.480 --> 00:13:27.300
"You know, that Thingus Racamagoocus had a lot of nice pictures,

00:13:27.400 --> 00:13:29.240
but way too many big words."

00:13:29.380 --> 00:13:33.860
So he led J Jonah Jameson to believe Strahm
was guilty, while leading Strahm to a seemingly

00:13:33.869 --> 00:13:35.310
inconspicuous house.

00:13:35.310 --> 00:13:38.339
A house that is the last house on the left
side of it’s street.

00:13:38.339 --> 00:13:41.180
With a light on the left and a storage area
on the right.

00:13:41.180 --> 00:13:43.389
A house with a small door underneath the stairs.

00:13:43.389 --> 00:13:46.550
A house whose halls have a very familiar layout.

00:13:46.550 --> 00:13:50.279
Could this location be the infamous Nerve
Gas House which was the venue of the main

00:13:50.279 --> 00:13:51.730
game seen in Saw 2?

00:13:51.730 --> 00:13:55.499
It’s definitely very similar in layout,
right down to the room that contained the

00:13:55.499 --> 00:13:59.351
safe, with a trap door in the same spot leading
down to the underground tunnel network, which

00:13:59.351 --> 00:14:02.910
appears to be the very same tunnel network
that contains the bathroom, judging by the

00:14:02.910 --> 00:14:04.819
inclusion of this sliding door.

00:14:04.819 --> 00:14:08.699
We know that John was a civil engineer once
involved in a major housing project and he

00:14:08.699 --> 00:14:10.800
does have access to multiple properties.

00:14:10.800 --> 00:14:14.959
In Saw 2 we see him utilize both the Nerve
Gas House and the Fake-Out House with a seemingly

00:14:14.959 --> 00:14:16.139
similar layout.

00:14:16.139 --> 00:14:20.029
We also know that the project was supposed
to provide housing to 40 low-income families,

00:14:20.029 --> 00:14:23.500
so it seems reasonable that each of these
units was based on the same design, and they

00:14:23.500 --> 00:14:24.790
may each have the same layout.

00:14:24.790 --> 00:14:28.389
I have to admit, it doesn’t really make
sense for John or Hoffman to pull off a major

00:14:28.389 --> 00:14:32.240
renovation of the dilapidated Nerve Gas House
just for this one small moment.

00:14:32.240 --> 00:14:35.200
I’m not really even sure that they could
fix up the entire house in such a short time

00:14:35.200 --> 00:14:40.399
frame, especially given all they accomplished
between October 2005 and April 2006, and Saw

00:14:40.400 --> 00:14:44.140
5 takes place not too long after, most likely
in July 2006.

00:14:44.140 --> 00:14:47.560
Maybe these houses were all placed on a grid,
so that there were several houses on the left

00:14:47.570 --> 00:14:49.209
side at the end of their respective streets.

00:14:49.209 --> 00:14:52.519
That would put the Hoffman House in the same
neighborhood as the Nerve Gas House and The

00:14:52.519 --> 00:14:56.220
Fake-Out House, which would make it plausible
that it could be connected to the same tunnel

00:14:56.220 --> 00:14:59.620
network that contains both The Bathroom and
the Glass Coffin room.

00:14:59.620 --> 00:15:03.459
And we know that it’s the same tunnel network,
because Strahm finds a big red streak, which

00:15:03.459 --> 00:15:07.670
we learn the origin of in Saw 7 when we see
the quote unquote “extended version” of

00:15:07.670 --> 00:15:10.040
Gordon’s escape from The Bathroom after
Saw 1.

00:15:10.040 --> 00:15:13.560
Like I mentioned earlier, this might just
be one of those Saw mysteries that can never

00:15:13.560 --> 00:15:16.900
be fully explained, forever being discussed
in the comments, although I think there is

00:15:16.900 --> 00:15:21.379
more of a chance that this location could
be revisited in a future movie, so maybe one

00:15:21.380 --> 00:15:22.160
day we’ll find out.

00:15:22.160 --> 00:15:25.580
The safe room is also full of easter eggs,
and I’m not even talking about this clown

00:15:25.589 --> 00:15:29.350
that is basically identical to that one clown
in Beverly’s house in the 2017 version of

00:15:29.350 --> 00:15:30.350
It.

00:15:30.350 --> 00:15:33.639
The room contains more of those Chinese figures,
the pig ornament from Jeff Denlon’s study,

00:15:33.639 --> 00:15:37.519
and several wood mannequins, a connection
to the many mannequins John used to test his

00:15:37.520 --> 00:15:41.860
traps on, dating back to the old Stygian Street
hideout in an old mannequin factory.

00:15:41.860 --> 00:15:44.500
So Hoffman’s plan, at least for now, works.

00:15:44.509 --> 00:15:47.939
J Jonah Erickson finds Strahm’s phone in
the catacombs at the site of the game, and

00:15:47.939 --> 00:15:51.689
Strahm falls victim to the collapsing walls
of The Glass Coffin Trap.

00:15:51.689 --> 00:15:55.410
At the beginning of this video I mentioned
the Pendulum, which was also the titular device

00:15:55.410 --> 00:15:58.269
in Edgar Allen Poe’s short story, The Pit
and Pendulum.

00:15:58.269 --> 00:16:02.470
To bring that connection full circle, Saw
5’s final trap represents The Pit.

00:16:02.470 --> 00:16:03.670
As written by Poe himself:

00:16:03.670 --> 00:16:07.850
“Its centre, and of course, its greatest
width, came just over the yawning gulf.

00:16:07.850 --> 00:16:11.470
I shrank back -- but the closing walls pressed
me resistlessly onward.

00:16:11.470 --> 00:16:15.300
At length for my seared and writhing body
there was no longer an inch of foothold on

00:16:15.300 --> 00:16:16.769
the firm floor of the prison.

00:16:16.769 --> 00:16:22.350
I struggled no more, but the agony of my soul
found vent in one loud, long, and final scream

00:16:22.350 --> 00:16:23.399
of despair.”

00:16:23.400 --> 00:16:27.280
While this would be the end of Strahm, his
legacy would live on in the sequel, so join

00:16:27.280 --> 00:16:31.480
me in the next episode for even more Things
You Missed in Saw 6.

00:16:31.489 --> 00:16:35.569
Remember to subscribe to CZsWorld for new
horrors every week, ring the deathbell for

00:16:35.569 --> 00:16:38.739
notifications and I’ll see you in the next
one.

00:16:38.739 --> 00:16:40.470
Assuming we both survive.

